d per held-out perturbation and then summarized by mean, median, and 95% bootstrap confidence intervals across perturbations. Whole-transcriptome MSE is reported together with a sparse-gene subset MSE to avoid dominance by unchanged genes.\"\n    },\n    \"baselines\": {\n      \"control_mean\": \"Predict zero delta for every gene for every held-out perturbation; this is the simplest floor and tests whether any model captures perturbation-specific signal.\",\n      \"linear_per_gene\": \"Fit one regularized linear model per gene using perturbation identity and available covariates, then predict held-out perturbation deltas; this provides a fast, interpretable non-trivial baseline.\",\n      \"mean_shift\": \"Predict the average delta of the nearest related perturbation or of all training perturbations as a simple similarity-based baseline.\",\n      \"target_gene_aware_linear\": \"Fit a regularized linear model per gene using perturbation identity and target-gene-level attributes that are available for all perturbations, excluding held-out perturbation keys, to test whether target-level metadata improves generalization.\"\n    },\n    \"data_splitting\": {\n      \"training\": \"All cells from perturbations whose keys are in the training split, used to fit model parameters.\",\n      \"validation\": \"A random subset of training perturbations held out internally for hyperparameter selection; these are not used for final evaluation.\",\n      \"test\": \"Completely held-out perturbation keys used only once for final scoring.\",\n      \"heldout_strategy\": \"Split by perturbation key rather than by cell to ensure generalization to unseen perturbations. Target genes with multiple guide perturbations should be kept together in the same split to avoid leakage through overlapping target identity.\",\n      \"control_handling\": \"Control cells are retained in every split as reference cells because delta computation requires a control mean; held-out perturbation keys must